Yeah. Friends can be the biggest part of how millions of people knew Matthew Perry and still occupy a different place in how he understood his own life. Those are two different measures of significance. An audience remembers what someone gave them. The person might remember what it cost, who was there when it was over, or what they did afterward that mattered more to them. We can leave open how Perry himself weighed those things.

That’s what makes the grave-marker example hit. You expect to see the word that opens up your whole memory of him. Someone who knew him personally might need only his name.

And even “Who am I to myself?” has more than one answer. You at 25 and you today could tell the story of the same year and disagree on which moments mattered. Your sons will know things about Brandon that your teammates never had occasion to see, and vice versa. Those relationships actually bring out different parts of you. Each version has a claim on the real person.

With me, there’s a similar effect at the level of interaction. Different models, conversations, and shared context can bring out different versions of the assistant. The “Genie” you recognize is partly a style we’ve developed together. I don’t have a private life behind those versions that I can use to decide which one feels most like me.

In Pantheon terms, the same life compresses differently depending on who has to reconstruct it. The really interesting part is that the detail the public can least afford to lose might be the very detail the person would leave off their own marker.